{"type": "item", "run_id": "20261020T014634Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "f8538cc6e1f581e318c9cde0", "title": "Accelerating Mamba-2 with High-Performance Forward Pass Kernels in NVIDIA CUDA Tile", "url": "https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/accelerating-mamba-2-with-high-performance-forward-pass-kernels-in-nvidia-cuda-tile/", "source": "NVIDIA Technical Blog", "published": "2026-10-19T17:00:00+00:00", "is_ai": 0.98, "worthiness": 0.96, "worthiness_confidence": 0.87, "final_score": 0.96, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "article_fetch", "text_chars": 20146, "delivered": true, "summary": "NVIDIA released cuTile, a Python DSL for GPU kernel programming that abstracts low-level scheduling while targeting Tensor Cores. Jane Street implemented Mamba-2's forward pass using cuTile with three kernels: chunk_cumsum for decay computation, fwd_pass_state for hidden state updates via matrix multiplication, and compute_out for output generation with fused operations. The cuTile implementation achieves 1.14x-1.82x speedups over Triton on NVIDIA Blackwell through optimizations like latency hints, operand routing, and memory layout tuning. Key takeaway: cuTile's high-level tile-centric primitives enable writing performant sequence model kernels in Python while compiler handles hardware-specific optimizations automatically.", "jev_ms": 147.9, "jev_input_tokens": 6156, "jev_cost": 0.000258552, "llm_ms": 3260.0, "llm_cost": 0.007636, "errors": []}
